      "claim": "PLAN: Gallery section (three photo cards) under the home page hero — planning only, nothing implemented.\n\n## What the repo actually looks like (evidence)\n\n- Tracked files are exactly `index.html`, `about.html`, `styles.css` (249 bytes). No build tooling, no JS, no `images/` directory.\n- `index.html` has no formal `<header>`/hero element. What reads as the \"hero\" is the top of `<main>`: `<h1>Tideline Woodworks</h1>`, `<p class=\"tagline\">Furniture made on the coast</p>`, and the intro paragraph `Hand-built tables and shelves…`. The gallery would slot between that paragraph and `</main>`.\n- No existing image usage: a repo-wide grep for `<img`, `<picture`, `<figure`, `background-image` returns nothing, and no `.jpg/.png/.webp/.svg/.gif/.avif` files exist. Every image the gallery needs must come from somewhere new.\n- `styles.css` is minimal (page max-width `40rem`, nav link spacing, footer rule, `.tagline` italic). It has no grid/flex or card styles, so card layout CSS is entirely new — and `styles.css` is shared by both pages, which constrains how we add to it.\n- Branch is byte-identical to `main` (`git diff main` empty). Note: Ship's prior-run history mentions a `contact.html` and an \"Our story\" nav label, but neither exists in this tree — this plan is based on the current two-page state and stays inside `index.html`/`styles.css`, so it would not conflict with those changes if they land first.\n\n## Assumptions (stated, not decided for you)\n\n1. \"Hero\" means the `h1` + `.tagline` + intro paragraph block at the top of `<main>` in `index.html`; the gallery goes directly after the intro paragraph, before `</main>`.\n2. \"Photo card\" means a `<figure>` containing an `<img>` plus a short `<figcaption>` caption. Semantic `<figure>`/`<figcaption>` beats a bare `<div>` here: the caption is a caption, screen readers get the pairing for free, and no extra class plumbing is needed.\n3. Cards are static (no lightbox, no links).\n4. Editing `styles.css` is in scope even though it is shared — additions will be class-scoped and purely additive, so `about.html` rendering is provably unchanged.\n5. No new page is created and the nav is untouched.\n\n## Open questions (genuinely change the work)\n\n- **Where do the three photos come from?** Nothing exists in the repo. Options: (a) you supply real files, (b) neutral placeholder images committed as `images/gallery-1.jpg`… to be swapped later, (c) no image files at all — colored CSS placeholder blocks. This is the biggest fork in the plan and I have not assumed an answer for a real implementation; for scoping, (b) is what the steps below assume.\n- **Image dimensions/aspect ratio** — ideally uniform (e.g. 4:3, ~800px wide) so three cards align without cropping hacks.\n- **Caption and alt-text wording** (e.g. product names like \"Driftwood dining table\") — needs your copy.\n- **Section heading** — include an `<h2>` (e.g. \"Recent work\") or let the cards stand alone?\n\n## Scoped steps (not executed)\n\n1. **Assets** — add `images/` with three photos, named consistently (e.g. `gallery-table.jpg`, `gallery-shelf.jpg`, `gallery-bench.jpg`).\n2. **`index.html`** — one insertion between the intro paragraph and `</main>`: a `<section class=\"gallery\">` (optionally with an `<h2>`) containing three `<figure class=\"card\">` elements, each with `<img src=\"images/…\" alt=\"…\" loading=\"lazy\">` and a `<figcaption>`. No other lines touched.\n3. **`styles.css`** — append only: `.gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(10rem, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 2rem; }` plus card framing (border, small padding) and `img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }`. Nothing existing is edited or removed.\n4. **Verify** — serve locally (`python3 -m http.server`) and check both pages; confirm no 404s for the images; check a narrow (~360px) viewport to confirm the cards wrap.\n\n## Tradeoffs in plain language\n\n- **Real photos vs placeholders vs CSS blocks:** real photos look right immediately but require assets I don't have; placeholder files keep the layout shippable and swappable; CSS-only blocks avoid binary files entirely but arguably aren't \"photo cards\". Cheapest honest option is placeholder files with clearly-named paths.\n- **`auto-fit` grid vs media queries:** `auto-fit` gives you a 3-up row on desktop and automatic stacking on phones in one rule with no breakpoints — right for a site this size. Media queries would buy finer control nobody has asked for.\n- **Shared stylesheet vs page-local styles:** the site's convention is one shared `styles.css`; adding page-scoped rules there is correct. The cost is that the file affects both pages, which is why the plan insists on additive, `.gallery`/`.card`-scoped rules only — `about.html` has no matching elements, so its rendering cannot change.\n- **`loading=\"lazy\"`:** free win for images below the fold, one attribute, no downside at this scale.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `index.html` contains one `<section class=\"gallery\">` placed after the intro paragraph and before `</main>`, holding exactly three `<figure>` cards.\n2. Every `<img>` has a non-empty `alt` and a resolvable `src` (no 404s when served); each card has a `<figcaption>`.\n3. `about.html` is byte-identical to its current version; `git diff` shows changes only in `index.html`, `styles.css`, and new files under `images/`.\n4. In `styles.css`, existing rules are untouched — the diff is purely appended lines scoped to `.gallery`/`.card`.\n5. At the 40rem page width the three cards sit in a row; at ~360px they stack one-per-row without horizontal scrolling.\n6. Both pages still load with no console errors via a local static server.\n\nNo implementation, merge, or deployment is authorized by this plan; it awaits your explicit go-ahead, and answers to the open questions above (especially the photo source) before any code is written.\nFINDINGS_JSON []\n[]",
